Core Transformations: How to Use
1. Translation (Moving the Network)
When to use: Reposition your network to align with real-world coordinates or other datasets.
Methods: - Direct Input: Enter exact X and Y values in the translation boxes - Arrow Buttons: Click ↑ ↓ ← → buttons to move by the step increment - Keyboard: Use arrow keys when no input field is selected - Click & Drag: Enable “Enable Drag” and double-click on plot/map, then drag
Pro Tips: - Set a smaller Step value for precise positioning - Use both visualization modes to verify placement against real geography - X = East/West, Y = North/South (standard Cartesian)
2. Rotation (Spinning the Network)
When to use: Align your network with true north, match satellite imagery orientation, or correct import rotation errors.
Methods: - Dial Control: Drag the circular dial for visual rotation - Numeric Input: Type exact degrees (0-360) in the angle box - Combined: Both controls are synchronized
Rotation Center Options: - Origin (0,0): Rotate around the coordinate system origin - Network Center: Rotate around the network’s geographic center (recommended) - Custom: Specify exact X,Y coordinates as pivot point
Pro Tips: - Use Network Center for most rotations to keep network centered - For 90° corrections, enter exact values: 90, 180, 270 - Check “Both” visualization mode to verify alignment with map imagery
3. Reflection (Mirroring)
When to use: Correct inverted networks, create symmetrical designs, or match mirrored coordinate systems.
Options: - Flip X: Mirror horizontally (left ↔ right) - Flip Y: Mirror vertically (top ↔ bottom) - Both: Combine for 180° rotation effect
Important: Reflections occur around the network’s center point, not the origin.
4. Scaling (Resizing)
When to use: Convert between unit systems (feet ↔ meters), adjust imported scale errors, or create design variations.
How it works: - Factor = 1.0: Original size - Factor > 1.0: Enlarge (2.0 = double size) - Factor < 1.0: Shrink (0.5 = half size) - Factor negative: Flip and scale
Note: Pipe lengths and other attributes are not automatically scaled - this only affects coordinates.
